window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["jenkins"]=[{"id":"jenkins-f0001","card":"q-jenkins-001-what-jenkins-is-and-the-devops-lifecycle","sec":"jenkins-s002","q":"What is Jenkins, and what role does it play in a CI/CD pipeline?","a":"<p>Jenkins is an open-source automation server written in Java. In CI/CD it is the orchestrator: it watches source control, builds and tests code on every change (CI), and automates packaging, artifact publishing and deployment through stages to various environments (CD). It ties together compilers, test runners, scanners and deploy tools via plugins and pipelines.</p>\n<p>The DevOps lifecycle is drawn as an infinity symbol: a continuous loop until the system is decommissioned. It has two groups of stages:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Development: Plan, Code, Build, Test.</li>\n  <li>Operational: Release, Deploy, Operate, Monitor.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Jenkins automates the Build, Test, Release and Deploy stages.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Integration</strong> ties to Build and Test. The goal is to find and fix issues early. Jenkins builds the code into an artifact (container image, Java archive, Windows executable), runs automated tests, and lets the artifact move on only when the tests pass.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery/Deployment</strong> ties to Release and Deploy. Release means uploading artifacts (push a container image to a repository, publish a JAR). Deploy can be manual or fully automated (Continuous Deployment), where Jenkins executes the deployment instructions with little or no human interaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Plan&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Planning the application...&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Code&#x27;) {\n            steps { git &#x27;https://github.com/example/repo.git&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn clean package&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;mvn test&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Release&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;docker push my-app:latest&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) {\n            steps { sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml&#x27; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0002","card":"q-jenkins-002-ci-vs-continuous-delivery-vs-continuous","sec":"jenkins-s003","q":"What is the difference between Continuous Integration, Continuous Delivery, and Continuous Deployment?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>CI</strong>: developers merge frequently; each change is automatically built and tested.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Delivery</strong>: every passing build is automatically prepared and deployable, but a human approves the release to production.</li>\n  <li><strong>Continuous Deployment</strong>: every passing change is released to production automatically, with no manual gate.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0003","card":"q-jenkins-003-jenkins-vs-github-actions","sec":"jenkins-s004","q":"What are the main advantages of GitHub Actions over tools like Jenkins or CircleCI?","a":"<p>GitHub integration, easy YAML setup, the action marketplace, a free tier and scalable runners.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0004","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Explain the controller-agent architecture in Jenkins.","a":"<p>The <strong>controller</strong> hosts the UI, schedules builds, stores config and job history, and manages plugins. <strong>Agents</strong> (nodes) are separate machines or containers that run the build workloads. The controller dispatches jobs to agents based on labels and available executors. Best practice: run no builds on the controller; keep it for orchestration only.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0005","card":"q-jenkins-004-controller-and-agent-architecture","sec":"jenkins-s005","q":"Why should you avoid running build workloads on the controller?","a":"<p>The controller holds all state, credentials and orchestration. Running builds there risks resource exhaustion (OOM/CPU), lets build scripts read sensitive controller files and secrets, and can crash the whole instance. Set controller executors to 0 and run all builds on agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0006","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What is a Jenkins executor, and how many per agent?","a":"<p>An executor is a slot on a node that runs one build at a time; a node with N executors runs N concurrent builds. Rule of thumb: about one executor per CPU core, fewer for memory-heavy or I/O-heavy builds. Too many causes contention or OOM; too few wastes capacity. Keep the controller's executors at 0.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0007","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are Jenkins labels?","a":"<p>Tags assigned to agents (for example <code>linux</code>, <code>docker</code>, <code>maven</code>). A pipeline requests a label so the build runs only on matching agents. Labels support expressions such as <code>linux &amp;&amp; docker</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { label &#x27;linux &amp;&amp; docker&#x27; }   // run only on agents with both labels\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0008","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"How does Jenkins authenticate agents, and which protocols are used?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>SSH</strong>: the controller connects out to the agent using SSH credentials (controller-initiated).</li>\n  <li><strong>Inbound (JNLP)</strong>: the agent connects in to the controller using a secret or agent token (agent-initiated; good behind NAT or firewalls).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Agents are validated through these secrets and keys, and you can also lock down which agent commands the controller trusts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0009","card":"q-jenkins-005-executors-labels-and-agent-protocols","sec":"jenkins-s006","q":"What are the default ports?","a":"<p>Web UI: 8080 (HTTP). The inbound (JNLP) agent port is configured separately (often 50000) under Manage Jenkins, Security; it can be fixed or random.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0010","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What is the difference between a Freestyle job and a Pipeline job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Freestyle</strong>: configured in the GUI, roughly single-stage; simple but limited, and the config lives inside Jenkins (harder to version and review).</li>\n  <li><strong>Pipeline</strong>: defined as code in a <code>Jenkinsfile</code> (Groovy); supports multi-stage flows, parallelism, <code>when</code> and <code>input</code>; lives in source control (auditable, reusable, portable).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0011","card":"q-jenkins-006-freestyle-vs-pipeline-jobs-and-plugins","sec":"jenkins-s007","q":"What are Jenkins plugins, and how do they extend core functionality?","a":"<p>Plugins are add-ons for SCM integrations (Git), build tools, pipeline steps, credential providers, cloud agents (Kubernetes, EC2), notifications and more. Jenkins core is intentionally minimal; almost all real functionality comes from plugins, managed under Manage Jenkins, Plugins.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0012","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"What is <code>JENKINS_HOME</code>, and what lives in it?","a":"<p>The root data directory holding all Jenkins state:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>config.xml</code>: global config</li>\n  <li><code>jobs/</code>: per-job config and build history</li>\n  <li><code>plugins/</code>: installed plugins</li>\n  <li><code>secrets/</code>: encryption keys and credential material</li>\n  <li><code>users/</code>, <code>nodes/</code>, <code>workspace/</code>, <code>fingerprints/</code>, <code>updates/</code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Backing it up backs up the whole instance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0013","card":"q-jenkins-007-jenkins-home-configuration-and-backup","sec":"jenkins-s008","q":"How does Jenkins store configuration, and why XML?","a":"<p>Config is persisted as XML files on disk (<code>config.xml</code>, per-job <code>config.xml</code>, node and user configs). Jenkins serializes its Java object model with XStream, so the files are human-readable, diffable and directly editable. That is also why config can be version-controlled and reloaded.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0014","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you back up and restore a Jenkins instance?","a":"<p>Manual: stop or quiesce Jenkins and archive <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> (at minimum <code>config.xml</code>, <code>jobs/</code>, <code>users/</code>, <code>secrets/</code>, <code>credentials.xml</code>, <code>plugins/</code>). To restore, put it back and restart. With plugins: <strong>ThinBackup</strong> (scheduled full or incremental), or keep config in JCasC plus jobs as code so the instance is reproducible.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0015","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"How do you move jobs from one Jenkins instance to another?","a":"<p>Copy the job folder under <code>jobs/&lt;name&gt;/</code> (with <code>config.xml</code>) to the new controller and reload; use the Jenkins CLI (<code>get-job</code> / <code>create-job</code>); use the Job Import plugin; or, best, keep jobs as <code>Jenkinsfile</code> and JCasC so migration is just pointing the new controller at the repos.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0016","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the difference between soft reload (<code>/reload</code>) and safe restart (<code>/safeRestart</code>)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>/reload</code> re-reads config from disk into memory without restarting the JVM (fast; use after editing config on disk).</li>\n  <li><code>/safeRestart</code> waits for running builds to finish, then restarts the JVM, so no builds are killed.</li>\n  <li><code>/restart</code> restarts immediately.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0017","card":"q-jenkins-008-backup-migration-and-restarts","sec":"jenkins-s009","q":"What is the System Log for, and where do you configure custom log recorders?","a":"<p>The System Log surfaces controller and plugin logging for diagnostics. Under Manage Jenkins, System Log you can add custom Log Recorders that capture specific loggers (by package or class) at chosen levels, which helps debug one plugin without drowning in global logs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0018","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"Which software stack does the Jenkins server setup use?","a":"<p>Ubuntu 20.04 LTS, OpenJDK 11 and Jenkins 2.332.1, with NGINX as a reverse proxy in front of Jenkins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0019","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"Why should you host Jenkins on a public cloud (AWS, Google Cloud, Azure)?","a":"<p>GitHub webhooks need a public URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0020","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"What can a local Jenkins install (Windows, macOS, Linux) do and not do?","a":"<p>It works for manual job runs but cannot receive webhooks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0021","card":"q-jenkins-009-requirements-and-where-to-host","sec":"jenkins-s010","q":"How long does the whole Jenkins server setup take?","a":"<p>About 15 minutes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0022","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"What EC2 instance setup does Jenkins use?","a":"<p>An Ubuntu Server 20.04 LTS AMI and type <code>t2.micro</code> (free tier). Raise the root volume to 30 GB for NGINX, Jenkins, plugins and Docker images. Tag it <code>Name: Jenkins server</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0023","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"Which security group rule does the Jenkins server need?","a":"<p>A security group <code>Jenkins server</code> allowing HTTP port 80 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (GitHub and browser access).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0024","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"Why allocate an Elastic IP for the Jenkins instance?","a":"<p>To associate it with the instance for a fixed address and persistent DNS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0025","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"How do you connect to the Jenkins EC2 instance?","a":"<p>Create an RSA key pair, download it and protect the file. Then run <code>chmod 600 jenkins-server.pem</code>, <code>ssh -i jenkins-server.pem ubuntu@&lt;public-dns&gt;</code> and <code>sudo su -</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0026","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"What does the Jenkins <code>install.sh</code> script do, and how do you run it?","a":"<p>Run it with <code>source install.sh</code>. It updates Ubuntu, installs NGINX and Jenkins, installs the suggested plugins, skips the setup wizard and prints the admin credentials.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0027","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"What do you do after the install script finishes?","a":"<p>Open the public DNS in a browser, log in with the printed admin password, optionally create a new admin user, and accept the default Jenkins URL. Then verify that NGINX proxies requests to Jenkins on port 8080.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0028","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"Which AWS CLI commands create the Jenkins security group and open port 80?","a":"<p><code>create-security-group</code>, then <code>authorize-security-group-ingress</code> for TCP port 80 from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 create-security-group --group-name JenkinsServer --description &quot;Jenkins server security group&quot;\naws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-name JenkinsServer --protocol tcp --port 80 --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0029","card":"q-jenkins-010-launching-jenkins-on-aws-ec2","sec":"jenkins-s011","q":"How do you add an optional SSH rule to the Jenkins security group?","a":"<p>Authorize TCP port 22. Tighten the CIDR for stricter access:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>aws ec2 authorize-security-group-ingress --group-name JenkinsServer --protocol tcp --port 22 --cidr 0.0.0.0/0</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0030","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"How does the install script install Java and Jenkins?","a":"<p>It updates Ubuntu, installs <code>openjdk-11-jdk</code>, adds the Jenkins repository and key, then installs <code>jenkins</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apt-get install -y openjdk-11-jdk\nwget -q -O - https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io.key | apt-key add -\necho &quot;deb https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/&quot; &gt; /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list\napt-get update -y\napt-get install -y jenkins</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0031","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"How does the install script set up NGINX as a reverse proxy for Jenkins?","a":"<p>It installs NGINX and writes a server block that listens on port 80 and proxies to <code>http://localhost:8080</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apt-get install -y nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name jenkins.example.com;\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://localhost:8080;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0032","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"Which headers does the NGINX config for Jenkins set?","a":"<p><code>Host</code>, <code>X-Real-IP</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>proxy_set_header Host \\$host;\nproxy_set_header X-Real-IP \\$remote_addr;\nproxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For \\$proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0033","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"How does the install script enable the NGINX site and start the services?","a":"<p>It links the site into <code>sites-enabled</code>, restarts NGINX, then enables and starts Jenkins:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/jenkins /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\nsystemctl restart nginx\nsystemctl enable jenkins\nsystemctl start jenkins</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0034","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"Where does the install script read the initial Jenkins admin password from?","a":"<p>From <code>/var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>echo &quot;Admin password: $(cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword)&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0035","card":"q-jenkins-011-install-script-jenkins-behind-nginx","sec":"jenkins-s012","q":"What must you replace in the install script, and how does the other variant differ?","a":"<p>Replace <code>jenkins.example.com</code> with the server's public DNS or IP. The earlier variant used the <code>https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian/</code> repository instead of <code>debian-stable</code>. Everything else is the same.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0036","card":"q-jenkins-012-your-first-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s013","q":"How do you create the first Hello World pipeline in Jenkins?","a":"<p>New Item, enter a name, choose <strong>Pipeline</strong>, then under Pipeline pick Try Sample Pipeline, Hello World. Save and click Build Now.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0037","card":"q-jenkins-012-your-first-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s013","q":"What does the Hello World declarative pipeline look like?","a":"<p>One <code>Hello</code> stage with an <code>echo</code> step:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Hello&#x27;) {\n            steps {\n                echo &#x27;Hello World&#x27;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0038","card":"q-jenkins-012-your-first-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s013","q":"What does <code>agent any</code> mean in a Jenkins pipeline?","a":"<p>The pipeline runs on any available executor.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0039","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"What are the key differences between Declarative and Scripted Pipeline syntax?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Declarative</strong>: opinionated and structured (<code>pipeline { ... }</code>), easier to read, with built-in <code>post</code>, <code>when</code> and <code>options</code>, validated up front. Preferred for most cases.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scripted</strong>: full Groovy (<code>node { ... }</code>), imperative, maximum flexibility but more complex and error-prone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can drop into scripted logic inside declarative with a <code>script {}</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Declarative\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;){ steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } } } }\n// Scripted\nnode { stage(&#x27;B&#x27;) { sh &#x27;make&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0040","card":"q-jenkins-013-scripted-vs-declarative-and-the-required","sec":"jenkins-s014","q":"Explain the core sections of a Declarative Pipeline.","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>pipeline</code>: the root block.</li>\n  <li><code>agent</code>: where it runs. <code>any</code> (any available executor), <code>label '...'</code> (a specific agent for an OS or tool), <code>docker</code> (inside a container for consistent environments), <code>none</code> (defer agent choice to individual stages).</li>\n  <li><code>stages</code>: container for one or more <code>stage</code>s (at least one required), such as Build, Test, Deploy.</li>\n  <li><code>stage</code>: a named phase.</li>\n  <li><code>steps</code>: the commands that do the work inside a stage (<code>sh</code>, <code>echo</code>); several commands are allowed per step block.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0041","card":"q-jenkins-014-a-multi-stage-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s015","q":"How do you define a multi-stage Jenkins pipeline with Build, Test, Deploy and Release?","a":"<p>Define four <code>stage</code> blocks inside <code>stages</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Building the application...&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Deploying to staging...&#x27; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Release&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &#x27;Releasing to production...&#x27; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0042","card":"q-jenkins-014-a-multi-stage-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s015","q":"How can a Jenkins stage run several commands?","a":"<p>Put several steps in its <code>steps</code> block, such as three <code>echo</code> lines in the Test stage:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>echo &#x27;Running unit tests...&#x27;\necho &#x27;Running integration tests...&#x27;\necho &#x27;Running end-to-end tests...&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0043","card":"q-jenkins-014-a-multi-stage-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s015","q":"What does Jenkins show for a multi-stage pipeline run?","a":"<p>A graphical view of the stages. Hover over a stage to see its logs and click a step for detailed logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0044","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What is the Jenkins snippet generator?","a":"<p>A tool that writes step code for you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0045","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"Which basic steps does the snippet generator commonly cover?","a":"<p><code>echo</code>, <code>git</code> (check out a repo), <code>sh</code> (run shell commands) and <code>archiveArtifacts</code> (store job artifacts).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0046","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"Where can you find the full list of Jenkins pipeline steps?","a":"<p>In the Jenkins docs under Basic Steps Reference and Pipeline Steps Reference (which includes plugin steps).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0047","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What are the steps to generate a snippet with the Jenkins snippet generator?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>In a pipeline job click the <strong>Pipeline Syntax</strong> link under the script field.</li>\n  <li>Pick a step from the Sample Step dropdown (for example <code>archiveArtifacts</code>) and set its parameters (for example <code>*.txt</code>).</li>\n  <li>Expand Advanced for options such as allow empty archive, only if successful, fingerprint.</li>\n  <li>Click Generate Pipeline Script, copy the snippet into the pipeline, use Apply to check syntax, then save and build.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0048","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What does the Declarative Directive Generator do?","a":"<p>It does the same as the snippet generator, for directives such as <code>parameters</code> and <code>tools</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0049","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"What does a pipeline that creates a file and archives it look like?","a":"<p>A Create File stage with <code>sh</code>, then an Archive stage with <code>archiveArtifacts</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;echo &quot;Sample report&quot; &gt; report.txt&#x27; }\narchiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;*.txt&#x27;, allowEmptyArchive: true, onlyIfSuccessful: true, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0050","card":"q-jenkins-015-pipeline-syntax-and-the-snippet-generato","sec":"jenkins-s016","q":"How do you know the archive step succeeded?","a":"<p>A blue icon appears next to the build, and <code>report.txt</code> is downloadable from the job page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0051","card":"q-jenkins-016-environment-variables-and-build-variable","sec":"jenkins-s017","q":"How do you define environment variables globally and at stage level?","a":"<p>Use an <code>environment</code> block at pipeline level (all stages) or inside a stage (that stage only). Reference with <code>${VAR}</code> or <code>env.VAR</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Name variables in capitals (<code>MAX_SIZE</code>) to tell them apart from lowercase keywords.</li>\n  <li>In strings prefix with <code>$</code> (<code>$VAR</code> or <code>${VAR}</code>), and stay consistent in the style you pick.</li>\n  <li>A stage-level definition overrides the global one for that stage only.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MAX_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n        MIN_SIZE = &#x27;1&#x27;\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Default Scale&#x27;) {\n            steps { echo &quot;Default: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n        stage(&#x27;Scale by 10X&#x27;) {\n            environment {\n                MAX_SIZE = &#x27;100&#x27;\n                MIN_SIZE = &#x27;10&#x27;\n            }\n            steps { echo &quot;Scaled: Max size is ${MAX_SIZE}, Min size is ${MIN_SIZE}&quot; }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The first stage prints 10 and 1, the second prints 100 and 10. The Pipeline Steps view shows each step's output clearly.</p>\n<p><strong>Current build variables</strong> are properties of the <code>currentBuild</code> object, accessed as <code>currentBuild.PROPERTY</code> (case-sensitive CamelCase): <code>currentBuild.startTime</code>, <code>currentBuild.duration</code>, <code>currentBuild.currentResult</code>. Use them for decisions based on build state, for example <code>echo \"Build duration: ${currentBuild.duration}\"</code>. The full list of default environment and <code>currentBuild</code> variables is under Pipeline Syntax, Global Variables Reference.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0052","card":"q-jenkins-017-the-post-section","sec":"jenkins-s018","q":"What is the <code>post</code> section, and which conditions can it evaluate?","a":"<p><code>post</code> runs after a stage or the whole pipeline based on outcome: <code>always</code>, <code>success</code>, <code>failure</code>, <code>unstable</code>, <code>changed</code> (result differs from the last run), <code>aborted</code>, <code>fixed</code>, <code>regression</code>. It suits notifications and cleanup.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always  { cleanWs() }\n  success { echo &#x27;OK&#x27; }\n  failure { mail to: &#x27;team@x.com&#x27;, subject: &quot;Failed: ${env.JOB_NAME}&quot;, body: &quot;${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0053","card":"q-jenkins-018-the-script-block","sec":"jenkins-s019","q":"What is the <code>script</code> block, and when should you use it?","a":"<p><code>script { }</code> embeds arbitrary Scripted (Groovy) code inside a declarative stage, for loops, complex conditionals or variable manipulation that declarative syntax cannot express. Use it sparingly; prefer shared-library steps for anything reusable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  script {\n    def services = [&#x27;api&#x27;, &#x27;web&#x27;, &#x27;worker&#x27;]\n    services.each { s -&gt; sh &quot;docker build -t ${s} ./${s}&quot; }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0054","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"How do you run stages in parallel?","a":"<p>Wrap stages in a <code>parallel</code> block so they run concurrently on available executors or agents, which cuts wall-clock time for independent work.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Tests&#x27;) {\n  parallel {\n    stage(&#x27;Unit&#x27;)        { steps { sh &#x27;make unit&#x27; } }\n    stage(&#x27;Integration&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make integration&#x27; } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0055","card":"q-jenkins-019-parallel-stages-and-matrix","sec":"jenkins-s020","q":"What is the <code>matrix</code> directive?","a":"<p>It runs a stage's steps across combinations of <code>axes</code> (for example OS by JDK version) in parallel without repeating stage code, ideal for cross-platform or multi-version testing. <code>excludes</code> skips invalid combinations.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>matrix {\n  axes {\n    axis { name &#x27;OS&#x27;;  values &#x27;linux&#x27;, &#x27;windows&#x27; }\n    axis { name &#x27;JDK&#x27;; values &#x27;11&#x27;, &#x27;17&#x27; }\n  }\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) { steps { echo &quot;Test on ${OS} JDK${JDK}&quot; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0056","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"How do <code>timeout</code>, <code>retry</code> and <code>catchError</code> work?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>timeout</code>: abort a block that exceeds a duration.</li>\n  <li><code>retry(n)</code>: re-run a block up to n times on failure.</li>\n  <li><code>catchError</code>: continue the pipeline but set the build result (for example UNSTABLE) instead of failing hard.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps {\n  retry(3) { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./flaky.sh&#x27; } }\n  catchError(buildResult: &#x27;UNSTABLE&#x27;, stageResult: &#x27;FAILURE&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./optional.sh&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0057","card":"q-jenkins-020-timeout-retry-catcherror-and-platform-st","sec":"jenkins-s021","q":"What is the difference between <code>sh</code>, <code>bat</code> and <code>powershell</code> steps?","a":"<p><code>sh</code> runs shell commands on Unix, Linux and macOS agents; <code>bat</code> runs Windows batch and <code>powershell</code> runs PowerShell on Windows agents. Pick by agent OS (target it with labels): <code>sh</code> will not work on a Windows agent and vice versa.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;          // Linux agent\n    // bat &#x27;mvn -B clean package&#x27;      // Windows agent\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0058","card":"q-jenkins-021-replay-and-restart-from-a-stage","sec":"jenkins-s022","q":"How do you replay or restart a build from a failed stage?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Replay</strong> re-runs a build with an editable copy of the pipeline script (good for quick experiments).</li>\n  <li><strong>Restart from Stage</strong> (declarative pipelines, with <code>preserveStashes</code>) restarts a completed run from a chosen stage, reusing earlier stashed artifacts so earlier successful stages are not repeated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are available from the build page in Blue Ocean or the classic UI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { preserveStashes(buildCount: 5) }   // enables reliable restart-from-stage</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0059","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"What are Jenkins pipeline parameters?","a":"<p>Variables that receive values when a job is triggered. They live in a <code>parameters</code> block at the top of the pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0060","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"How do you read a pipeline parameter in Jenkins?","a":"<p>With <code>params.NAME</code> (or just <code>NAME</code>). In strings use <code>$NAME</code> or <code>${NAME}</code>. Use capitals for names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0061","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"What does each Jenkins parameter need?","a":"<p>A name, a default value and a description.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0062","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"What are the Jenkins parameter types?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>string</strong>: single-line text field.</li>\n  <li><strong>text</strong>: multi-line text field.</li>\n  <li><strong>booleanParam</strong>: checkbox.</li>\n  <li><strong>choice</strong>: dropdown; the first option is the default.</li>\n  <li><strong>password</strong>: masked input for sensitive data such as API keys.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0063","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"How do you declare string, text, boolean, choice and password parameters?","a":"<p>Inside a <code>parameters</code> block:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parameters {\n    string(name: &#x27;USERNAME&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;guest&#x27;, description: &#x27;Enter your username&#x27;)\n    text(name: &#x27;BIO&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;No bio provided&#x27;, description: &#x27;Enter your bio&#x27;)\n    booleanParam(name: &#x27;DEBUG&#x27;, defaultValue: false, description: &#x27;Enable debug mode&#x27;)\n    choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;dev&#x27;, &#x27;staging&#x27;, &#x27;prod&#x27;], description: &#x27;Select environment&#x27;)\n    password(name: &#x27;API_KEY&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;secret123&#x27;, description: &#x27;Enter API key&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0064","card":"q-jenkins-022-pipeline-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s023","q":"How do you print the parameter values in a Jenkins stage?","a":"<p>Use <code>echo</code> with <code>${params.NAME}</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>echo &quot;Username: ${params.USERNAME}&quot;\necho &quot;Debug Mode: ${params.DEBUG}&quot;\necho &quot;Environment: ${params.ENVIRONMENT}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0065","card":"q-jenkins-023-first-build-and-build-with-parameters","sec":"jenkins-s024","q":"How do you configure parameterised builds (String, Choice, Boolean, Git Parameter)?","a":"<p>Declare a <code>parameters</code> block; values are supplied at build time and read via <code>params.NAME</code>. The Git Parameter plugin adds a <code>gitParameter</code> type for choosing branches or tags.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parameters {\n  string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;1.0.0&#x27;, description: &#x27;Release version&#x27;)\n  choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;dev&#x27;,&#x27;staging&#x27;,&#x27;prod&#x27;], description: &#x27;Target&#x27;)\n  booleanParam(name: &#x27;RUN_TESTS&#x27;, defaultValue: true, description: &#x27;Run tests?&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0066","card":"q-jenkins-024-conditional-stages-with-when","sec":"jenkins-s025","q":"How do you run a stage conditionally with <code>when</code>?","a":"<p><code>when</code> decides whether a stage runs. If it evaluates to true the stage runs, otherwise it is skipped. Conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>branch</code>: only for given Git branches (for example <code>main</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>environment</code>: an environment variable has a given value.</li>\n  <li><code>expression</code>: a Groovy expression using parameters or variables (for example <code>params.ENV != 'prod'</code>).</li>\n  <li>Others include <code>changeRequest</code> and <code>tag</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Deploy Prod&#x27;) {\n  when { branch &#x27;main&#x27;; expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n  steps { echo &#x27;Deploying to production&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0067","card":"q-jenkins-025-manual-approvals-with-input","sec":"jenkins-s026","q":"How do you require a manual approval before production?","a":"<p>The <code>input</code> step pauses the pipeline for a human to Proceed or Abort, with a custom message to explain the action. Wrap it in <code>timeout</code> so it cannot hang forever.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Approve&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    timeout(time: 15, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n      input message: &#x27;Deploy to production?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Deploy&#x27;\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The paused stage shows a confirmation dialog when you hover over it in the pipeline view.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0068","card":"q-jenkins-026-conditional-deploy-example","sec":"jenkins-s027","q":"How does a pipeline pick the deploy stage by environment?","a":"<p>It declares a <code>choice</code> parameter <code>ENVIRONMENT</code> and uses <code>when { expression { ... } }</code> on each deploy stage:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>parameters {\n    choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;dev&#x27;, &#x27;staging&#x27;, &#x27;prod&#x27;], description: &#x27;Select deployment environment&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0069","card":"q-jenkins-026-conditional-deploy-example","sec":"jenkins-s027","q":"How do the non-production and production deploy stages differ in the conditional deploy pipeline?","a":"<p>Non-production runs when the environment is not <code>prod</code>. Production runs when it is <code>prod</code> and asks for confirmation with <code>input</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Deploy to Non-Production&#x27;) {\n    when { expression { params.ENVIRONMENT != &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n    steps { echo &quot;Deploying to ${params.ENVIRONMENT} environment...&quot; }\n}\nstage(&#x27;Deploy to Production&#x27;) {\n    when { expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;prod&#x27; } }\n    steps {\n        input message: &#x27;Confirm deployment to production?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Deploy&#x27;\n        echo &quot;Deploying to production...&quot;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0070","card":"q-jenkins-026-conditional-deploy-example","sec":"jenkins-s027","q":"What happens in the conditional deploy pipeline for <code>dev</code> and for <code>prod</code>?","a":"<p>For <code>dev</code> or <code>staging</code> the production stage is skipped and the non-production stage deploys automatically. For <code>prod</code> the pipeline pauses at the <code>input</code> step until someone clicks Deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0071","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"What are the requirements of the parameterized pipeline exercise?","a":"<p>Parameters <code>ENVIRONMENT</code> (choice: development, staging, production), <code>API_KEY</code> (password, default <code>123ABC</code>) and <code>CHANGELOG</code> (text). Stages Test, Deploy and Report. Deploy runs only for <code>production</code>. Report writes the changelog to a file named after the environment and archives it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0072","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"Which tool helps write the parameter snippets in the exercise, and what is a common mistake with <code>choices</code>?","a":"<p>Use the Declarative Directive Generator. The <code>choices</code> must be separate strings in a list (<code>['development', 'staging', 'production']</code>), not one string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0073","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"How are the exercise's three parameters declared?","a":"<p>Choice, password and text:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>choice(name: &#x27;ENVIRONMENT&#x27;, choices: [&#x27;development&#x27;, &#x27;staging&#x27;, &#x27;production&#x27;], description: &#x27;Select an environment for deployment&#x27;)\npassword(name: &#x27;API_KEY&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;123ABC&#x27;, description: &#x27;Enter the API key&#x27;)\ntext(name: &#x27;CHANGELOG&#x27;, defaultValue: &#x27;&#x27;, description: &#x27;Enter the changelog&#x27;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0074","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"How does the Report stage write and archive the changelog file named after the environment?","a":"<p>It writes the text with <code>sh</code>, then archives the file:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &quot;echo &#x27;${params.CHANGELOG}&#x27; &gt; ${params.ENVIRONMENT}.txt&quot;\narchiveArtifacts artifacts: &quot;${params.ENVIRONMENT}.txt&quot;, allowEmptyArchive: true</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0075","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"How do you make the Deploy stage run only for production?","a":"<p>Add a <code>when</code> expression:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>when { expression { params.ENVIRONMENT == &#x27;production&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0076","card":"q-jenkins-027-parameterized-pipeline-exercise","sec":"jenkins-s028","q":"What are the test results of the parameterized pipeline exercise?","a":"<p>With <code>development</code>, Deploy is skipped and <code>development.txt</code> holds the changelog. With <code>production</code>, Deploy runs and <code>production.txt</code> holds it. The API key shows as dots in the UI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0077","card":"q-jenkins-028-jenkinsfile-and-pipeline-as-code","sec":"jenkins-s029","q":"What is a Jenkinsfile, and why store it in source control?","a":"<p>A <code>Jenkinsfile</code> is a text file defining the pipeline, committed alongside the code. Benefits: version control and code review, auditable history, reproducibility across branches and environments, self-documenting pipelines, and Multibranch pipelines that build every branch and PR automatically. It also supports a <strong>GitOps</strong> approach where the repository is the single source of truth for application code, tool configuration and CI changes, which can be reviewed and merged through automated processes. A Jenkinsfile can hold tools, options, triggers and more.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0078","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"Which SCMs does Jenkins support, and which plugin does Git need?","a":"<p>Several SCMs (Git, Subversion). Git is the most popular and needs the Git plugin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0079","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"What are the steps to set up Pipeline script from SCM in Jenkins?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Choose <strong>Pipeline script from SCM</strong> in the job.</li>\n  <li>Pick the SCM (Git).</li>\n  <li>Enter the repository URL and credentials if the repo is private (public repos need none).</li>\n  <li>Set the branch specifier (set it to the repo's default branch, for example <code>main</code>).</li>\n  <li>Set the script path to the Jenkinsfile (root by default).</li>\n  <li>Save and build.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0080","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"What does a pipeline with a Checkout stage using <code>git</code> look like?","a":"<p>The Checkout stage calls <code>git</code> with <code>branch</code> and <code>url</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Checkout&#x27;) {\n    steps { git branch: &#x27;main&#x27;, url: &#x27;https://your-repo-url.git&#x27; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0081","card":"q-jenkins-029-pipeline-script-from-scm","sec":"jenkins-s030","q":"Where does Jenkins take the project configuration from when using Pipeline script from SCM?","a":"<p>Jenkins checks out the repository and takes the project configuration from the Jenkinsfile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0082","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What are the ways to trigger a Jenkins job?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Webhooks</strong>: the SCM pushes an event and the build starts instantly (best).</li>\n  <li><strong>Poll SCM</strong>: Jenkins periodically checks the repo for changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scheduled (cron)</strong>: build on a time schedule regardless of changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Remote API or token</strong>: trigger over HTTP (<code>buildWithParameters</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Upstream</strong>: build after another project completes.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  githubPush()                 // webhook\n  pollSCM(&#x27;H/5 * * * *&#x27;)       // poll every ~5 min\n  cron(&#x27;H 2 * * *&#x27;)            // nightly ~2am\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0083","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>pollSCM</code> and <code>cron</code>?","a":"<p><code>pollSCM('schedule')</code> checks SCM on that schedule and builds only if there are changes. <code>cron('schedule')</code> builds unconditionally on the schedule (nightly jobs). Use <code>H</code> (hash) to spread load and avoid every job firing on the hour.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0084","card":"q-jenkins-030-ways-to-trigger-a-job","sec":"jenkins-s031","q":"How do GitHub or GitLab webhooks trigger builds instantly?","a":"<p>The SCM sends an HTTP POST to Jenkins (<code>/github-webhook/</code> or the GitLab hook URL) on push or PR events. Jenkins matches it to jobs watching that repo and builds immediately, which is faster and cheaper than polling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0085","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is a Multibranch Pipeline?","a":"<p>It scans a repo and creates and runs a pipeline for every branch and PR that contains a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>. New branches create new jobs and deleted branches remove them. Each branch builds with its own Jenkinsfile, so per-branch CI needs no manual job setup.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0086","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"What is the GitHub Organization Folder or GitLab Group plugin?","a":"<p>They scan an entire GitHub org or GitLab group and auto-create a Multibranch Pipeline for every repo containing a Jenkinsfile. New repos are picked up on the next scan or webhook.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0087","card":"q-jenkins-031-multibranch-pipelines-and-organization-f","sec":"jenkins-s032","q":"How do you build pull requests and report status back?","a":"<p>Use a Multibranch Pipeline with the GitHub or GitLab Branch Source: it discovers PRs and builds them, and Jenkins reports commit status (pending, success, failure) to the PR automatically (or with <code>githubNotify</code> or the GitLab status). Combine with branch protection to require the check before merge.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0088","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger downstream jobs and pass parameters?","a":"<p>Use the <code>build</code> step with <code>parameters</code>. <code>wait</code> and <code>propagate</code> control blocking and result propagation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>build job: &#x27;deploy-service&#x27;,\n  parameters: [string(name: &#x27;VERSION&#x27;, value: env.BUILD_NUMBER)],\n  wait: true, propagate: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0089","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"How do you trigger a build remotely with cURL and an API token?","a":"<p>Create a user API token, then POST to the job's <code>build</code> or <code>buildWithParameters</code> endpoint (with a crumb if CSRF protection is on). Trigger tokens can also be set per job.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>curl -X POST &quot;https://jenkins.example.com/job/my-job/buildWithParameters?VERSION=1.2.3&quot; \\\n  --user &quot;alice:11aabbccddeeff...&quot;   # user:apiToken</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0090","card":"q-jenkins-032-downstream-jobs-remote-triggers-and-webh","sec":"jenkins-s033","q":"What is the Generic Webhook Trigger plugin?","a":"<p>It lets any system trigger a job through one webhook URL and extracts fields from the JSON or XML payload into variables with JSONPath. The variables can feed an optional filter that decides whether to build.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>triggers {\n  GenericTrigger(\n    genericVariables: [[key: &#x27;ref&#x27;, value: &#x27;$.ref&#x27;]],\n    token: &#x27;my-token&#x27;,\n    regexpFilterText: &#x27;$ref&#x27;, regexpFilterExpression: &#x27;refs/heads/main&#x27;\n  )\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0091","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is Git sparse checkout, and how do you speed up large checkouts?","a":"<p>Sparse checkout fetches only specified paths instead of the whole repo, which helps monorepos. Other options: shallow clone (<code>depth</code>), skipping tags, reference repos, and disabling unneeded submodules.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;,\n  branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [\n    [$class: &#x27;CloneOption&#x27;, depth: 1, shallow: true, noTags: true],\n    [$class: &#x27;SparseCheckoutPaths&#x27;, sparseCheckoutPaths: [[path: &#x27;services/api/&#x27;]]]\n  ],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/monorepo.git&#x27;]]\n])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0092","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you handle Git submodules during checkout?","a":"<p>Enable the <code>SubmoduleOption</code> extension (recursive, with parent credentials) so submodules are initialized and updated.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>checkout([$class: &#x27;GitSCM&#x27;, branches: [[name: &#x27;*/main&#x27;]],\n  extensions: [[$class: &#x27;SubmoduleOption&#x27;, recursiveSubmodules: true, parentCredentials: true]],\n  userRemoteConfigs: [[url: &#x27;https://github.com/org/repo.git&#x27;]]])</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0093","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"How do you stop concurrent builds of the same branch?","a":"<p>Add <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code>: a new build of the same job or branch queues until the current one finishes, avoiding races on shared resources and deploys. <code>abortPrevious: true</code> cancels the older run instead.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { disableConcurrentBuilds() }\n// or: options { disableConcurrentBuilds(abortPrevious: true) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0094","card":"q-jenkins-033-checkout-optimization-concurrency-and-su","sec":"jenkins-s034","q":"What is the Quiet Period?","a":"<p>A configurable delay between a trigger firing and the build starting. It batches a burst of rapid commits into one build and avoids redundant runs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0095","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"Why keep complex build logic in scripts stored in the repo?","a":"<p>So the pipeline stays clear and easy to debug.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0096","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"What do <code>sh</code>, <code>bat</code> and <code>dir</code> do in a Jenkins pipeline?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>sh</code>: run commands or scripts with the default shell on Linux, Unix or macOS.</li>\n  <li><code>bat</code>: the Windows equivalent.</li>\n  <li><code>dir</code>: change the working directory for the steps inside it, with a path relative to the workspace or absolute. <code>${WORKSPACE}</code> gives the checkout directory.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0097","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"Which paths should you use for scripts inside and outside the repo?","a":"<p>Relative paths for scripts inside the repo and absolute paths for scripts outside the workspace, in the format the agent OS expects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0098","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"How do you make a repo script executable and run it with a relative path in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Use <code>chmod +x</code>, then run it with <code>./</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;chmod +x scripts/fibonacci.sh&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;./scripts/fibonacci.sh 60&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0099","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"How do you run a script with its full path using <code>${WORKSPACE}</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>sh</code> with <code>${WORKSPACE}</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;${WORKSPACE}/scripts/fibonacci.sh 60&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0100","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"How do you run a script from a subdirectory with <code>dir</code>?","a":"<p>Wrap the step in <code>dir('scripts')</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dir(&#x27;scripts&#x27;) {\n    sh &#x27;./fibonacci.sh 60&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0101","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"How does the Fibonacci Bash script print the sequence?","a":"<p>It loops <code>n</code> times, printing <code>a</code> and advancing the pair:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>n=$1\na=0\nb=1\nfor ((i=0; i&lt;n; i++)); do\n    echo $a\n    fn=$((a + b))\n    a=$b\n    b=$fn\ndone</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0102","card":"q-jenkins-034-running-scripts-from-a-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s035","q":"What do you check when a script stage fails in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Check that the script is executable and the path matches the agent's OS. Run the job once so Jenkins registers the parameter, then use Build with Parameters (for example <code>60</code>). All stages should print identical output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0103","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"What are the prerequisites for connecting Jenkins to GitHub?","a":"<p>A publicly reachable Jenkins server and a GitHub account.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0104","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"How do you prepare the GitHub repository for a Jenkins job?","a":"<p>Create or reuse a repository, add a <code>Jenkinsfile</code> (Add file, Create new file) and commit to <code>main</code>. Copy the HTTPS URL from the Code button.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0105","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"How do you create the Jenkins job for a GitHub repo?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Click New Item, name the job after the repo (for example <code>connect-jenkins-to-github</code>) and choose <strong>Pipeline</strong>.</li>\n  <li>General: optionally add the repo URL under <strong>GitHub project</strong> for a clickable link.</li>\n  <li>Build Triggers: enable <strong>GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Pipeline: select <strong>Pipeline script from SCM</strong>, SCM <code>Git</code>, paste the URL, set Branch Specifier to <code>main</code> and keep Script Path <code>Jenkinsfile</code>.</li>\n  <li>Save and click Build Now.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0106","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"Why is a first successful build required for a GitHub-connected Jenkins job?","a":"<p>So Jenkins reads the repo configuration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0107","card":"q-jenkins-035-prepare-the-repository-and-the-job","sec":"jenkins-s036","q":"What does a four-stage Jenkinsfile for the GitHub job look like?","a":"<p>Checkout, Build, Test and Deploy stages. Checkout uses <code>git</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { git branch: &#x27;main&#x27;, url: &#x27;https://github.com/your-username/connect-jenkins-to-github.git&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0108","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"How do you create the GitHub webhook for Jenkins?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Copy the Jenkins server URL (right-click the Jenkins icon, Copy Link Address).</li>\n  <li>In the repo go to Settings, Webhooks, Add webhook.</li>\n  <li>Payload URL: the Jenkins URL plus <code>/github-webhook/</code>.</li>\n  <li>Content type: <code>application/json</code>. Events: <strong>Just the push event</strong>. Click Add webhook.</li>\n  <li>GitHub pings Jenkins.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0109","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"What is the payload URL format of a GitHub webhook for Jenkins?","a":"<p>The Jenkins URL plus <code>/github-webhook/</code>, for example <code>http://your-jenkins-server/github-webhook/</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0110","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"How do you know the GitHub webhook works?","a":"<p>A green checkmark means the connection works. If not, edit or recreate the webhook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0111","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"How do you test that a push triggers a Jenkins build?","a":"<p>Edit <code>README.md</code> and commit to <code>main</code>. A new build should start by itself, and its log shows the commit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0112","card":"q-jenkins-036-create-the-github-webhook","sec":"jenkins-s037","q":"What do you check when a GitHub webhook does not trigger Jenkins?","a":"<p>The Jenkins server must be publicly reachable, the URL must end with <code>/github-webhook/</code>, the first Jenkins build must have succeeded, and the repo URL, branch specifier and webhook settings should be rechecked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0113","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"What is a Jenkins status badge?","a":"<p>A dynamic image showing whether a Jenkins job's build is passing or failing, usually placed in a repo <code>README.md</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0114","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"How do you add a status badge to a repository README?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Install the <strong>Embeddable Build Status</strong> plugin (Manage Jenkins, Manage Plugins, Available, search <code>embed</code>), choosing Download now and install after restart, and restart Jenkins.</li>\n  <li>Open the job, select <strong>Embeddable Build Status</strong>, choose the <strong>Markdown</strong> option and the <strong>Unprotected</strong> link, and copy the snippet.</li>\n  <li>Edit <code>README.md</code> in GitHub, paste the snippet near the top and commit to <code>main</code>.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0115","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"What does the Markdown snippet for a Jenkins status badge look like?","a":"<p>An image link to <code>buildStatus</code> that links to the job:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Markdown</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[![Build Status](http://your-jenkins-server/job/fibonacci/buildStatus)](http://your-jenkins-server/job/fibonacci/)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0116","card":"q-jenkins-037-status-badge-in-readme","sec":"jenkins-s038","q":"What do you check when a Jenkins status badge does not show?","a":"<p>Check the plugin is installed and Jenkins restarted, the snippet is correct, the Jenkins URL is reachable, and the job has run so the status is accurate.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0117","card":"q-jenkins-038-challenge-build-a-pi-script-on-every-pus","sec":"jenkins-s039","q":"What is the Pi challenge task in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Connect a repo (for example <code>pi-challenge</code>) holding a <code>Jenkinsfile</code> and <code>algorithm/pi.sh</code> to a Jenkins pipeline job, trigger a build on every push, and show the status badge in <code>README.md</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0118","card":"q-jenkins-038-challenge-build-a-pi-script-on-every-pus","sec":"jenkins-s039","q":"What are the steps of the Pi challenge?","a":"<p>Create the repo and job, enable the GitHub hook trigger, run a first build and add the webhook. Then edit <code>pi.sh</code> and commit (for example \"Improve calculation of pi\") to confirm a build fires with the new commit message. Finally install Embeddable Build Status and add the badge. The badge commit triggers another build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0119","card":"q-jenkins-038-challenge-build-a-pi-script-on-every-pus","sec":"jenkins-s039","q":"How does the Pi challenge pipeline run the script?","a":"<p>It makes <code>algorithm/pi.sh</code> executable, then runs it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &#x27;chmod +x algorithm/pi.sh&#x27;\nsh &#x27;./algorithm/pi.sh&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0120","card":"q-jenkins-038-challenge-build-a-pi-script-on-every-pus","sec":"jenkins-s039","q":"What does the Pi script print?","a":"<p>The value of Pi:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>echo &quot;Precise value of Pi: 3.141592653589793&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0121","card":"q-jenkins-039-default-security-realms-and-authorizatio","sec":"jenkins-s040","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with LDAP, Active Directory or SAML/Okta for SSO?","a":"<p>Set the Security Realm to LDAP, Active Directory, or a SAML/OIDC plugin. Jenkins delegates authentication to the identity provider, and group memberships flow in and map to authorization roles or the matrix. This centralizes identity and enables SSO.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0122","card":"q-jenkins-040-matrix-based-security","sec":"jenkins-s041","q":"What is the difference between matrix-based security and Role-Based Access Control (Role Strategy plugin)?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Matrix-based</strong>: a global grid mapping users and groups to permissions. Simple, but it does not scale because every user needs a row.</li>\n  <li><strong>Role Strategy (RBAC)</strong>: define reusable roles (global plus per-project or folder patterns) and assign users or groups to them; far more scalable for many users and teams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Configure matrix security:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Manage Users: create users (username, password, name, email).</li>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Configure Global Security: switch from \"Logged-in users can do anything\" to <strong>Matrix-based security</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Add your admin account and tick <strong>Administer</strong>. Add other users with specific permissions such as Overall Read (interact with the UI) and Job Build, Cancel, Read.</li>\n  <li>Save, then log in as the restricted user and confirm there is no management menu and only limited job actions.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Best practice: limit admin access to one or two users on a shared system and give everyone else role-based permissions. Always grant your own account Administer before saving, because locking yourself out is common (the Jenkins docs explain how to disable access control to recover).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0123","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"What is project-based matrix authorization in Jenkins?","a":"<p>It sets permissions per job or folder instead of globally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0124","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"How do you set up project-based permissions in Jenkins?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Configure Global Security, choose <strong>Project-based Matrix Authorization Strategy</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Recreate the matrix, since global settings do not carry over: admin with Overall Administer, other users with Overall Read.</li>\n  <li>Open a job (for example <code>complex-pipeline</code>), Configure, enable <strong>Project-based security</strong>, add the user and grant Build, Delete and so on.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0125","card":"q-jenkins-041-project-based-permissions","sec":"jenkins-s042","q":"What does a user see under project-based security?","a":"<p>Only the jobs they have rights on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0126","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"What is the goal of the Engineering and Finance Jenkins challenge?","a":"<p>Two teams whose users cannot touch each other's folders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0127","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"What are the steps to isolate the Engineering and Finance teams in Jenkins?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Create users <code>engineering</code> and <code>finance</code> (Manage Users, Create User).</li>\n  <li>Select Project-based Matrix Authorization Strategy; give <code>admin</code> Overall Administer and both team users Overall Read.</li>\n  <li>Create folders <code>Engineering Jobs</code> and <code>Finance Jobs</code> (New Item, Folder). In each, enable Project-based security and give the matching user all permissions.</li>\n  <li>Log in as <code>engineering</code>: only <code>Engineering Jobs</code> is visible, with no Manage Jenkins and no <code>Finance Jobs</code>. Repeat for <code>finance</code>.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0128","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"What warning may appear when configuring the team users, and how should it be treated?","a":"<p>A system-user warning may appear. It can be dismissed for the exercise but should be reviewed in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0129","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"Why is Overall Read required in a Jenkins permission matrix?","a":"<p>A user needs it to see anything at all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0130","card":"q-jenkins-042-challenge-isolate-engineering-and-financ","sec":"jenkins-s043","q":"What good practices apply to the folder isolation setup?","a":"<p>Test by logging in as each restricted user, use descriptive folder names, and back up the configuration to recover from lockouts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0131","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the Credentials provider, and which types can it store?","a":"<p>The Credentials plugin stores secrets encrypted in Jenkins and exposes them to jobs by ID, never as plain text. Types: username and password, SSH private key, secret text (API keys, tokens), secret file and certificates. Credentials can be domain-restricted.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0132","card":"q-jenkins-043-credentials-store-and-scopes","sec":"jenkins-s044","q":"What is the difference between credential scopes?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: usable by any job or pipeline on the instance.</li>\n  <li><strong>System</strong>: usable only by Jenkins itself (connecting to agents, email), not exposed to job builds.</li>\n  <li><strong>Item/Folder</strong>: scoped to one folder or job (least privilege, good for team isolation).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0133","card":"q-jenkins-044-using-credentials-in-a-jenkinsfile","sec":"jenkins-s045","q":"How do you inject credentials securely?","a":"<p><code>withCredentials</code> binds a stored credential to environment variables for the duration of a block, then unbinds it, and Jenkins masks the values in logs. Alternatively use <code>credentials('id')</code> in an <code>environment</code> block. For username and password credentials it sets one variable holding <code>username:password</code> plus two derived variables, <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_USR</code> and <code>&lt;NAME&gt;_PSW</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;dockerhub&#x27;,\n    usernameVariable: &#x27;USER&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;PASS&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$PASS&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}\n// or environment block:\nenvironment { AWS_KEY = credentials(&#x27;aws-access-key&#x27;) }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n    agent any\n    environment {\n        MY_CRED = credentials(&#x27;my-credential-id&#x27;)   // username:password\n    }\n    stages {\n        stage(&#x27;Example&#x27;) {\n            steps {\n                echo &quot;Username: ${env.MY_CRED_USR}&quot;  // MY_CRED_PSW holds the password\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Secret text with withCredentials\nwithCredentials([string(credentialsId: &#x27;my-api-key&#x27;, variable: &#x27;API_KEY&#x27;)]) {\n    sh &#x27;echo $API_KEY&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use the string form of <code>withCredentials</code> for secret text credentials. Recreate the permission matrix when switching strategies and test as a restricted user.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0134","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"Why never hardcode tokens or passwords in steps, and how do you mask output?","a":"<p>Hardcoded secrets leak into SCM, build logs and job config and cannot be rotated centrally. Use the Credentials store with <code>withCredentials</code> (Jenkins masks bound values in logs). The Mask Passwords plugin can redact custom secret strings. Do not <code>echo</code> secrets.</p>\n<p>Several credentials can be bound in one <code>withCredentials</code> block:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([\n  usernamePassword(credentialsId: &#x27;docker&#x27;, usernameVariable: &#x27;U&#x27;, passwordVariable: &#x27;P&#x27;),\n  string(credentialsId: &#x27;npm-token&#x27;, variable: &#x27;NPM_TOKEN&#x27;)\n]) {\n  sh &#x27;echo &quot;$P&quot; | docker login -u &quot;$U&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0135","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"What does the SSH Agent plugin do?","a":"<p>It starts an <code>ssh-agent</code> for a build and loads a stored SSH private-key credential into it, so <code>git</code>, <code>ssh</code> and <code>scp</code> authenticate without the key being written to disk. The key is available only inside the wrapped block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sshagent([&#x27;deploy-ssh-key&#x27;]) {\n  sh &#x27;ssh deploy@server &quot;systemctl restart app&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0136","card":"q-jenkins-045-secrets-hygiene-ssh-agent-and-vault","sec":"jenkins-s046","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins with Vault or AWS Secrets Manager?","a":"<p>Use the HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager Credentials Provider plugin. Secrets stay in the external manager (single source of truth, rotation, audit) and are fetched at runtime, so there are no long-lived copies in Jenkins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withVault(vaultSecrets: [[path: &#x27;secret/app&#x27;, secretValues: [[envVar: &#x27;DB_PASS&#x27;, vaultKey: &#x27;password&#x27;]]]]) {\n  sh &#x27;deploy --password &quot;$DB_PASS&quot;&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0137","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"What is Script Security, and why do some Groovy scripts need admin approval?","a":"<p>The Script Security plugin gates Groovy that runs on the controller. Sandboxed pipeline code is limited to safe APIs; anything outside the sandbox (or <code>@NonCPS</code> code using sensitive methods) goes to In-process Script Approval, where an admin must approve it. This stops arbitrary code from compromising the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0138","card":"q-jenkins-046-script-security-csrf-and-hardening","sec":"jenkins-s047","q":"How do you restrict script capabilities with the Groovy Sandbox?","a":"<p>Run pipeline Groovy in the sandbox (the default for a Jenkinsfile from SCM). Only allowlisted, safe methods are allowed; anything else is blocked or needs admin approval, so pipeline code cannot call dangerous controller APIs or the filesystem directly.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0139","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you protect the controller against CSRF?","a":"<p>Enable CSRF protection with the crumb issuer (on by default in modern Jenkins). State-changing requests must carry a valid crumb, which blocks forged cross-site requests. API clients fetch a crumb first, or use API tokens, which are crumb-exempt for scripted calls.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0140","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What is Agent-to-Controller security?","a":"<p>Agents (possibly less trusted) can ask the controller to run commands or read files. Agent-to-Controller security restricts this with a configurable allow and deny list of commands and file access, which limits the blast radius if an agent is compromised.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0141","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you disable anonymous read access?","a":"<p>In the authorization strategy (matrix or role), remove all permissions from the Anonymous user (do not grant Overall Read). Then only authenticated users can view Jenkins. Create a real admin account first so you do not lock yourself out.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0142","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"How do you audit user actions and job changes?","a":"<p>Install the <strong>Audit Trail</strong> plugin to log who did what (config changes, triggers, deletions) to a file, syslog or console. Combine it with <strong>Job Configuration History</strong> to track and diff config changes over time.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0143","card":"q-jenkins-047-csrf-agent-security-and-auditing","sec":"jenkins-s048","q":"What security considerations apply to untrusted code from GitHub PRs?","a":"<p>PRs from forks can carry a malicious Jenkinsfile or build scripts. Require approval to build fork PRs (use the trusted strategy under \"Discover PRs from forks\"), never expose production credentials to PR builds, run PR builds on isolated ephemeral agents, use least-privilege scoped credentials, and avoid sandbox bypass. Treat PR code as hostile.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0144","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"Why keep jobs off the Jenkins controller?","a":"<p>The controller provides the web UI for managing the server. Keeping jobs off it frees CPU, memory and disk for scheduling and management.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0145","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"What is the difference between a Jenkins node and an agent?","a":"<p>A <strong>node</strong> is a server connected to Jenkins. The <strong>agent</strong> is the process on it that runs job commands and reports status back. The two terms are often used interchangeably.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0146","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"What is a Jenkins SSH node?","a":"<p>A node that connects with a user and SSH key. It is secure and good for nodes outside the controller's network. It needs SSH access, a user and key for Jenkins, and a recent Java (the agent is a Java app). It works on any OS with SSH and Java.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0147","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"What is a Jenkins Docker agent?","a":"<p>An agent where each build runs in a new container, giving a fresh isolated environment. It needs Docker on the node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0148","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"How does a pipeline choose an agent when there are several nodes?","a":"<p><code>agent any</code> runs on the first available agent. With several nodes use labels to pick agents with the right capabilities:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent { label &#x27;maven-agent&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0149","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"How do you configure tools such as Maven for agents?","a":"<p>Configure tools under Manage Jenkins, Global Tool Configuration, with a name such as <code>Maven-3.8.4</code> and automatic install enabled, then reference them in the <code>tools</code> block. Jenkins provisions the right version on whichever agent runs the stage. For containers, bake tools into the agent image instead.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0150","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"Why does a pipeline on an agent need a checkout step?","a":"<p>The first checkout happens on the controller only to read the Jenkinsfile. Code is not automatically on the agent, so add a checkout step:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tools { maven &#x27;Maven-3.8.4&#x27; }\nsteps { checkout scm }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn clean install&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0151","card":"q-jenkins-048-nodes-agents-and-when-to-use-them","sec":"jenkins-s049","q":"How do you manage tool installations across different agents?","a":"<p>Define installers in Global Tool Configuration and reference them with a <code>tools</code> block:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tools { maven &#x27;Maven-3.9&#x27;; jdk &#x27;JDK-17&#x27; }   // names from Global Tool Config</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0152","card":"q-jenkins-049-pipeline-considerations-for-agents","sec":"jenkins-s050","q":"How do you manage tool installations across different agents?","a":"<p>Define installers in Global Tool Configuration and reference them with a <code>tools</code> block.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent any\n  tools { maven &#x27;Maven-3.9&#x27;; jdk &#x27;JDK-17&#x27; }   // names from Global Tool Config\n  stages { stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;mvn -v &amp;&amp; mvn package&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0153","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"What are the prerequisites for adding an SSH agent?","a":"<p>A server reachable by SSH from Jenkins, and a user and key (for example an EC2 instance).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0154","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"How do you create the SSH agent node in Jenkins?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Manage Jenkins, Manage Nodes and Clouds, <strong>New Node</strong>. Name it (for example <code>Linux</code>) and choose <strong>Permanent Agent</strong>.</li>\n  <li>Set the remote root directory (for example <code>/home/ec2-user</code>) and a label such as <code>linux</code>.</li>\n  <li>Usage: \"Only build jobs with label expressions matching this node\".</li>\n  <li>Launch method: <strong>Launch agents via SSH</strong>; Host: DNS name or IP.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0155","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"Which credentials does the SSH agent use?","a":"<p>Add a Jenkins credential of kind \"SSH Username with Private Key\", ID <code>linux</code>, username <code>ec2-user</code>, and paste the full private key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0156","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"Which host key and availability settings does the SSH agent use?","a":"<p>Host Key Verification Strategy: Non verifying (used in the course). Availability: keep the agent online as much as possible. Save to connect, then check the agent's Log for errors and confirm it is online.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0157","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"How does a pipeline run on the <code>linux</code> SSH agent?","a":"<p>With <code>agent { label 'linux' }</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent { label &#x27;linux&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0158","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"How do you confirm a job ran on the SSH agent?","a":"<p>Build Now, then check Console Output for the message showing the job ran on the <code>linux</code> agent and its workspace path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0159","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"When does a Git step on an agent need credentials?","a":"<p>Private repositories need Jenkins credentials. The public exercise repo does not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0160","card":"q-jenkins-050-adding-an-ssh-agent","sec":"jenkins-s051","q":"How does the Java build pipeline checkout and build on the SSH agent?","a":"<p>A Source stage with <code>git</code>, then Maven stages:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { git url: &#x27;https://github.com/your-repo/java-app.git&#x27;, credentialsId: &#x27;github-credentials&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn clean&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn test&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0161","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"What are the prerequisites for Docker agents in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Enough storage and RAM on the Jenkins server for Docker images, Docker installed as a service (check <code>docker --version</code> and <code>docker ps</code>), and the <strong>Docker Pipeline</strong> plugin installed (Manage Plugins, Installed) to enable the <code>docker</code> agent keyword.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0162","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"How do you run a pipeline in a Maven Docker container?","a":"<p>Use the <code>docker</code> agent with <code>image</code> and <code>args</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n    docker {\n        image &#x27;maven:latest&#x27;\n        args &#x27;--name maven-container&#x27;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0163","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"Why pin a Docker image version in a Jenkins <code>docker</code> agent?","a":"<p>Pin a version (for example <code>maven:3.8.4</code>) instead of <code>latest</code> for consistency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0164","card":"q-jenkins-051-docker-agents","sec":"jenkins-s052","q":"What does the Console Output show for a Docker agent build?","a":"<p>Docker pulling and running the image, the commands inside the container, then the container being stopped and removed. Every run starts a fresh container, though the image may be reused.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0165","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"Why does a Hugo (Go) build in a <code>golang</code> Docker agent take about 4 minutes every run?","a":"<p>The Go cache sits in <code>/tmp</code> inside the container, which is destroyed after each build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0166","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"How does a workspace cache fix a slow Docker agent build?","a":"<p>Jenkins mounts the workspace as a volume, so moving the cache there persists it. Set <code>GOCACHE</code> to the workspace path:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>environment {\n    GOCACHE = &#x27;/var/jenkins_home/workspace/hugo-pipeline&#x27;   // was &#x27;/tmp&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0167","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"Which Docker agent does the Hugo build use?","a":"<p>The <code>golang:latest</code> image, named <code>go-container</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n    docker {\n        image &#x27;golang:latest&#x27;\n        args &#x27;--name go-container&#x27;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0168","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"How much faster is the second Hugo build with the workspace cache?","a":"<p>The first run builds the cache (about 4 minutes). The second reuses it and drops to about 17 seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0169","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"Why might the workspace path in <code>GOCACHE</code> differ?","a":"<p>The workspace path may vary, so use the path shown in the pipeline configuration or logs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0170","card":"q-jenkins-052-speeding-up-a-docker-agent-build-with-a","sec":"jenkins-s053","q":"Which stages build and check Hugo in the pipeline?","a":"<p>Checkout with <code>git</code>, Build with <code>go build</code> and Test with <code>./hugo version</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;go build&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;./hugo version&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0171","card":"q-jenkins-053-kubernetes-and-cloud-agents","sec":"jenkins-s054","q":"What is the Kubernetes plugin, and how does it spin up agents?","a":"<p>It runs each build in an ephemeral pod created on demand in a cluster and deletes it afterwards. You define a cloud pointing at the cluster and pod templates, and Jenkins scales agents elastically with zero idle cost, ideal for bursty CI.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes {\n    yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n      apiVersion: v1\n      kind: Pod\n      spec:\n        containers:\n        - name: maven\n          image: maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17\n          command: [&#x27;sleep&#x27;]\n          args: [&#x27;9999999&#x27;]\n    &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0172","card":"q-jenkins-054-ephemeral-agents","sec":"jenkins-s055","q":"Why are dynamic ephemeral agents ideal for the cloud?","a":"<p>They are created per build and destroyed after, so there are no long-lived nodes to patch and no leaked state. Benefits: a clean reproducible environment per run, elastic scale (pay only while building) and no snowflake agents. They suit Kubernetes and EC2, where compute is on demand.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0173","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you run several containers (Maven, AWS CLI) in one pod?","a":"<p>Declare multiple containers in the pod YAML and target each with <code>container('name')</code>; they share the workspace volume.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>agent {\n  kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven, image: maven:3.9, command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: aws,   image: amazon/aws-cli, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; }\n}\nstages {\n  stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;)  { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n  stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;aws&#x27;)   { sh &#x27;aws s3 cp target/app.jar s3://bucket/&#x27; } } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0174","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How do you provision EC2 instances as agents with the AWS EC2 plugin?","a":"<p>Configure an EC2 cloud with an AMI, instance type, IAM role, subnet and security group, and labels. When jobs need capacity Jenkins launches EC2 instances as agents (over SSH) and terminates idle ones per the retention strategy.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0175","card":"q-jenkins-055-multi-container-pods-ec2-agents-and-kani","sec":"jenkins-s056","q":"How does workspace persistence work in Kubernetes pods?","a":"<p>By default an ephemeral pod's workspace vanishes with the pod. Mount a PVC (or use <code>workspaceVolume: dynamicPVC(...)</code>) so the workspace persists across steps, containers or reruns, which helps caches such as <code>.m2</code> and <code>node_modules</code>. Trade-offs: PVC lifecycle and cleanup, and RWX access constraints.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0176","card":"q-jenkins-056-building-images-in-kubernetes-agents","sec":"jenkins-s057","q":"How do you run Docker-in-Docker or Kaniko in Kubernetes agents to build images?","a":"<p>DinD runs a Docker daemon in a sidecar and needs privileged mode, which is a security concern. Kaniko builds images in userspace without a Docker daemon or privileged access, so it is preferred in Kubernetes. It pushes straight to a registry using mounted credentials.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n  sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>A pod that builds with Maven and then pushes an image with Kaniko (the <code>debug</code> executor image provides a shell):</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>pipeline {\n  agent { kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - {name: maven,  image: maven:3.9,       command: [cat], tty: true}\n      - {name: kaniko, image: gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:debug, command: [cat], tty: true}\n  &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; } }\n  stages {\n    stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;maven&#x27;)  { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; } } }\n    stage(&#x27;Image&#x27;) { steps { container(&#x27;kaniko&#x27;) {\n      sh &#x27;/kaniko/executor --context `pwd` --dockerfile Dockerfile --destination reg/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    } } }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0177","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What is the difference between launch methods?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent via SSH</strong>: the controller SSHes out to start the agent. Simple for Linux hosts the controller can reach.</li>\n  <li><strong>Launch agent by connecting it to the controller (Inbound/JNLP)</strong>: the agent runs <code>agent.jar</code> and initiates the connection. Good behind NAT or firewalls, and for Windows.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cloud plugins</strong>: Kubernetes, EC2 or Docker create agents on demand.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The choice depends on network reachability, OS and whether agents are static or dynamic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0178","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"How do you set retention strategies for dynamic agents?","a":"<p>They control the agent lifecycle: <strong>Always</strong> (keep up), <strong>Demand</strong> (start when needed), <strong>Idle timeout</strong> (terminate after N idle minutes), or plugin-specific strategies. Short idle timeouts minimize cloud cost, and a small warm pool reduces cold-start latency.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0179","card":"q-jenkins-057-launch-methods-retention-and-offline-nod","sec":"jenkins-s058","q":"What does Node Offline mean, and what causes disconnects?","a":"<p>The controller lost contact with a node and cannot schedule builds there. Common causes: network or firewall issues, agent process crash or OOM, disk full on the agent, Java version mismatch, an expired credential or secret, or the node being taken offline for maintenance.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0180","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is a Shared Library, and why use it in enterprises?","a":"<p>A versioned Git repo of reusable pipeline code (steps, helpers, classes) that many Jenkinsfiles import. It enforces DRY, standardizes CI/CD across teams (one place to fix or upgrade), hides complexity behind simple custom steps and keeps each Jenkinsfile tiny.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0181","card":"q-jenkins-058-what-a-shared-library-is","sec":"jenkins-s059","q":"What is the structure of a Shared Library?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>vars/</code>: global custom steps and variables; each <code>foo.groovy</code> becomes a callable step <code>foo(...)</code>.</li>\n  <li><code>src/</code>: Groovy classes in package folders for OOP-style helper logic.</li>\n  <li><code>resources/</code>: non-Groovy files (templates, JSON, scripts) loaded with <code>libraryResource</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>(root)\n├── vars/          # deployApp.groovy  → step: deployApp(...)\n├── src/           # org/acme/Utils.groovy\n└── resources/     # org/acme/config.json</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0182","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you load a library implicitly vs explicitly?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Implicit</strong>: a Global Shared Library configured with \"Load implicitly\" is available in every pipeline.</li>\n  <li><strong>Explicit</strong>: <code>@Library('my-lib@main') _</code> at the top of the Jenkinsfile, pinning a branch, tag or commit.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@Library(&#x27;shared-pipeline@v2.1&#x27;) _\npipeline { agent any; stages { stage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ steps { deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27; } } } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0183","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"How do you write a custom step in <code>vars/</code> with an argument map?","a":"<p>Define a <code>call</code> method taking a <code>Map</code> of named arguments with defaults; the file name becomes the step name.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// vars/deployApp.groovy\ndef call(Map args = [:]) {\n  String env     = args.env ?: &#x27;dev&#x27;\n  String version = args.version ?: env.BUILD_NUMBER\n  echo &quot;Deploying ${version} to ${env}&quot;\n  sh &quot;./deploy.sh ${env} ${version}&quot;\n}\n// usage: deployApp env: &#x27;prod&#x27;, version: &#x27;1.4.0&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0184","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between <code>vars/*.groovy</code> globals and <code>src/</code> classes?","a":"<p><code>vars</code> files expose a step-like global (with <code>call()</code> making it invocable as <code>name(args)</code>), good for the pipeline-facing DSL. <code>src</code> classes are regular Groovy or OOP for structured, reusable and unit-testable logic. Use <code>vars</code> for the API surface and <code>src</code> for the implementation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0185","card":"q-jenkins-059-loading-a-library-and-writing-a-custom-s","sec":"jenkins-s060","q":"What is the difference between Global and Folder-level Shared Libraries?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Global</strong>: configured in Manage Jenkins, available instance-wide, can be trusted (runs outside the sandbox) and loaded implicitly.</li>\n  <li><strong>Folder-level</strong>: configured on a folder, scoped to jobs in it, always sandboxed and untrusted. Use for team-specific libraries and to limit the blast radius.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0186","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"Which Groovy concepts matter for robust pipelines (CPS, <code>@NonCPS</code>)?","a":"<p>Pipeline Groovy is CPS-transformed (continuation-passing style) so builds can pause, resume and survive controller restarts. That means program state must be serializable and some Groovy features behave oddly. Mark methods <code>@NonCPS</code> when they do complex non-pausing work (iterating collections with closures, using non-serializable objects), and keep pipeline steps out of them.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>@NonCPS\ndef parse(String json) { new groovy.json.JsonSlurper().parseText(json) }  // runs outside CPS</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0187","card":"q-jenkins-060-groovy-in-pipelines-cps-and-noncps","sec":"jenkins-s061","q":"What causes <code>NotSerializableException</code>, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>CPS serializes pipeline state at every step, so holding a non-serializable object (a <code>JsonSlurper</code> result type, a <code>Matcher</code>, streams) across a step boundary throws it. Fix by confining such objects to a <code>@NonCPS</code> method, nulling them before a step, or copying into serializable types (<code>HashMap</code>, <code>String</code>) before continuing.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0188","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you unit test shared libraries?","a":"<p><code>JenkinsPipelineUnit</code> mocks the pipeline runtime so library code runs in plain JUnit or Spock tests, asserting which steps were called and with which arguments, without a live Jenkins. Register mock steps, load the library, execute and verify the call stack.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>// Spock-style\ndef &quot;deployApp echoes target&quot;() {\n  when:  helper.callClosure(&#x27;deployApp&#x27;, [env: &#x27;prod&#x27;])\n  then:  helper.callStack.any { it.methodName == &#x27;echo&#x27; &amp;&amp; it.args[0].contains(&#x27;prod&#x27;) }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0189","card":"q-jenkins-061-testing-and-versioning-libraries","sec":"jenkins-s062","q":"How do you version and test library changes without breaking pipelines?","a":"<p>Keep the library in Git and pin versions in <code>@Library('lib@v1.3')</code> so consumers do not auto-adopt breaking changes. Develop on a branch, test with <code>@Library('lib@feature-x')</code> in a sandbox job (and JenkinsPipelineUnit), then release a tag and bump consumers deliberately. Semantic versioning and a changelog keep upgrades safe.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0190","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"How do you archive artifacts, and what is the difference between artifacts and stashes?","a":"<p><code>archiveArtifacts</code> saves files to the controller, viewable and downloadable from the build page for the build's retention. A <strong>stash</strong> is temporary in-build storage for passing files between stages or agents and is deleted when the build ends. Archive for deliverables, stash for hand-offs within the pipeline.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>archiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;target/*.jar&#x27;, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0191","card":"q-jenkins-062-archiving-artifacts","sec":"jenkins-s063","q":"What is the difference between <code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> and <code>archiveArtifacts</code>?","a":"<p><code>stash</code>/<code>unstash</code> move files between stages or agents within one build (ephemeral, not shown in the UI). <code>archiveArtifacts</code> persists files to the controller and exposes them in the UI and API.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Build&#x27;) { steps { sh &#x27;make&#x27;; stash name: &#x27;app&#x27;, includes: &#x27;dist/**&#x27; } }\nstage(&#x27;Deploy&#x27;){ agent { label &#x27;deployer&#x27; }; steps { unstash &#x27;app&#x27;; sh &#x27;./deploy.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0192","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"What does the Copy Artifact plugin do?","a":"<p>It lets one job read artifacts from another through the <code>copyArtifacts</code> step. Install it under Manage Jenkins, Plugins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0193","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"How does the job that creates an artifact allow another job to copy it?","a":"<p>It must explicitly grant permission with <code>copyArtifactPermission</code>, a comma-separated list such as <code>'job1,job2'</code>. <code>'*'</code> allows every job and should be used cautiously:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options {\n    copyArtifactPermission(&#x27;read-artifact-job&#x27;)\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0194","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"What error appears when <code>copyArtifactPermission</code> is missing?","a":"<p>The pipeline fails with <code>Permission to copy artifact is missing for create-artifact-job</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0195","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"How does Job A create and archive <code>report.txt</code>?","a":"<p>It writes the file with <code>writeFile</code> and archives it in <code>post { always { ... } }</code> with fingerprinting:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { writeFile file: &#x27;report.txt&#x27;, text: &#x27;Sample report content&#x27; }\narchiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;report.txt&#x27;, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0196","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"How does Job B copy the artifact from Job A?","a":"<p>With <code>copyArtifacts</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>copyArtifacts projectName: &#x27;create-artifact-job&#x27;,\n              selector: specific(&#x27;lastSuccessful&#x27;),\n              target: &#x27;copied_files&#x27;,\n              fingerprintArtifacts: true</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0197","card":"q-jenkins-063-sharing-artifacts-between-jobs","sec":"jenkins-s064","q":"What do the <code>copyArtifacts</code> parameters <code>projectName</code>, <code>selector</code>, <code>target</code> and <code>fingerprintArtifacts</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>projectName</code> is the source job. <code>selector</code> chooses the build (<code>lastSuccessful</code>, <code>lastCompleted</code>, or a specific build number). <code>target</code> is the workspace directory to copy into (default: workspace root). <code>fingerprintArtifacts</code> records the usage.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0198","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"What is fingerprinting in Jenkins?","a":"<p>It tracks which jobs created or used an artifact. Jenkins computes an MD5 checksum for each artifact and stores it with the job details in an internal database.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0199","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"Which options record artifact creation and use in fingerprinting?","a":"<p><code>fingerprint: true</code> on <code>archiveArtifacts</code> records creation. <code>fingerprintArtifacts: true</code> on <code>copyArtifacts</code> records each use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0200","card":"q-jenkins-064-fingerprinting","sec":"jenkins-s065","q":"How do you view an artifact's fingerprint in the Jenkins UI?","a":"<p>Open a build, choose <strong>See Fingerprints</strong>, and click the artifact (for example <code>report.txt</code>) to see the MD5 value and the jobs that used it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0201","card":"q-jenkins-065-junit-reports-and-code-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s066","q":"How do you publish JUnit results and coverage to the UI?","a":"<p>Use <code>junit</code> for test results (trends, and the build goes UNSTABLE on failures) and the coverage plugin (<code>recordCoverage</code>, or <code>publishHTML</code> for HTML reports).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  always {\n    junit &#x27;target/surefire-reports/*.xml&#x27;\n    recordCoverage tools: [[parser: &#x27;JACOCO&#x27;, pattern: &#x27;target/site/jacoco/jacoco.xml&#x27;]]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0202","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"What are the five stages of the Python Jenkins pipeline?","a":"<p>Setup (install dependencies), Lint (syntax), Test (JUnit report plus Cobertura coverage), Build and Deploy.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0203","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"What does the Jenkins server need for the Python pipeline?","a":"<p>Python 3 and <code>python3-venv</code> (Ubuntu), Git, and the JUnit and Code Coverage API plugins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0204","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"Which commands verify Python on the Jenkins server?","a":"<p><code>python3 --version</code> and <code>python3 -m venv test &amp;&amp; . test/bin/activate</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0205","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"Which commands do the Setup and Lint stages run?","a":"<p>Install requirements and run pylint:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;pip install -r requirements.txt&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;pylint *.py&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0206","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"How does the Test stage produce a JUnit report and Cobertura coverage?","a":"<p>It runs pytest with <code>--junitxml</code> and coverage options, then publishes in <code>post</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sh &#x27;pytest --junitxml=report.xml --cov=. --cov-report=xml:coverage.xml&#x27;\njunit &#x27;report.xml&#x27;\npublishCoverage adapters: [coberturaAdapter(&#x27;coverage.xml&#x27;)]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0207","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"Which commands do the Build and Deploy stages run?","a":"<p><code>python setup.py build</code> and an echo for deploying:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>steps { sh &#x27;python setup.py build&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;echo Deploying application&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0208","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"What changes when running the Python pipeline on Windows?","a":"<p>Install Python and Git manually and fix the Python and pip paths in the Jenkinsfile. Set the branch specifier to <code>main</code> and the script path to the Jenkinsfile location when using the exercise repo.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0209","card":"q-jenkins-066-python-pipeline-with-tests-and-coverage","sec":"jenkins-s067","q":"How do you view test and coverage reports in Jenkins?","a":"<p>Test results appear as a trend graph on the project page, and the Test Results link shows passes, failures and skips with package drill-down. Coverage shows a trend graph, a Coverage Report overview and package details, and a File Coverage tab with line-by-line source.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0210","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"What happens to a Jenkins pipeline when a test fails in the Test stage and results are published with <code>junit</code> in <code>post</code>?","a":"<p>The Test stage fails, the Build and Deploy stages are skipped, and the build is marked <strong>unstable</strong> (yellow).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0211","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"What does the Test Results link show for a failed test?","a":"<p>The failed test with its error message and stack trace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0212","card":"q-jenkins-067-what-a-failing-test-does-to-the-pipeline","sec":"jenkins-s068","q":"How do you publish JUnit results even when pytest fails?","a":"<p>Put <code>junit</code> in the stage's <code>post { always { ... } }</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Test&#x27;) {\n    steps { sh &#x27;pytest --junitxml=report.xml&#x27; }\n    post {\n        always { junit &#x27;report.xml&#x27; }\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0213","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"What is the goal of the Maven test report and JAR artifact challenge?","a":"<p>For a Java app, run tests and publish a JUnit report, build a JAR and archive it only when tests pass, without failing the build when results or artifacts are absent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0214","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"How do you prepare Jenkins for the Maven challenge?","a":"<p>Configure Maven under Global Tool Configuration (for example <code>Maven 384</code>), confirm the JUnit plugin is installed, and use the Declarative Directive Generator (Tools) and the snippet generator (<code>junit</code>, <code>archiveArtifacts</code>) to produce the pieces.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0215","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"Which stages does the Maven challenge pipeline have?","a":"<p>Clean, Test and Package, using the configured tool:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tools {\n    maven &#x27;Maven 384&#x27;\n}\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn clean&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn test&#x27; }\nsteps { sh &#x27;mvn package&#x27; }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0216","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"How does the <code>post</code> block publish the JUnit report and archive the JAR?","a":"<p>In <code>always</code> with <code>junit</code> and <code>archiveArtifacts</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>junit testResults: &#x27;**/TEST-com.learningjenkins.apptest.xml&#x27;, allowEmptyResults: true\narchiveArtifacts artifacts: &#x27;**/hello-1.0-SNAPSHOT.jar&#x27;, allowEmptyArchive: true, onlyIfSuccessful: true, fingerprint: true</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0217","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"What does <code>post { always { ... } }</code> do?","a":"<p>It runs regardless of success, failure or unstable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0218","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"What do <code>junit testResults</code> and <code>allowEmptyResults: true</code> do?","a":"<p><code>testResults</code> takes an Ant-style pattern; <code>**/</code> searches all directories. <code>allowEmptyResults: true</code> keeps a missing report from failing the build.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0219","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"What do the <code>archiveArtifacts</code> options <code>allowEmptyArchive</code>, <code>onlyIfSuccessful</code> and <code>fingerprint</code> do?","a":"<p><code>allowEmptyArchive: true</code> does not fail when no JAR exists. <code>onlyIfSuccessful: true</code> archives only from successful runs. <code>fingerprint: true</code> stores an MD5 so the artifact can be tracked across jobs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0220","card":"q-jenkins-068-challenge-maven-test-report-and-jar-arti","sec":"jenkins-s069","q":"What does a successful Maven challenge build show?","a":"<p>An artifact icon for the JAR, and repeated builds extend the test trend graph. The first run is slow because Maven is installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"jenkins-f0221","card":"q-jenkins-069-jenkins-cli","sec":"jenkins-s070","q":"What is the Jenkins CLI, and how do you run management commands?","a":"<p><code>jenkins-cli.jar</code> runs admin and job commands from a terminal or script: create and build jobs, manage nodes, reload config, safe-restart. Authenticate with an API token, over SSH or the HTTP endpoint.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>java -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN build my-job -f -v\njava -jar jenkins-cli.jar -s https://jenkins.example.com -auth alice:TOKEN safe-restart</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0222","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"What is JCasC, and how does it replace manual UI administration?","a":"<p>The <code>configuration-as-code</code> plugin defines the whole controller configuration (security realm, clouds, tools, credentials, global settings) in a declarative YAML file. Instead of clicking through Manage Jenkins, you version the YAML and apply it, which makes the controller reproducible, reviewable and recoverable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>jenkins:\n  systemMessage: &quot;Managed by JCasC&quot;\n  numExecutors: 0\n  securityRealm:\n    local: { allowsSignup: false }\n  authorizationStrategy:\n    roleBased: { roles: {} }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0223","card":"q-jenkins-070-configuration-as-code-jcasc","sec":"jenkins-s071","q":"How do you export the existing configuration to <code>jenkins.yaml</code>?","a":"<p>Go to Manage Jenkins, Configuration as Code, View/Download Configuration to export the live config as YAML. Trim it, move secrets to environment variables or <code>${VAR}</code> references, commit it, and point <code>CASC_JENKINS_CONFIG</code> at it so later startups load it.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0224","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you monitor controller health, CPU, memory and thread dumps?","a":"<p>Use the Monitoring (JavaMelody) plugin for CPU, heap and threads, the built-in System Information and Load Statistics, and take thread dumps at <code>/threadDump</code> (or via the CLI) to diagnose hangs. Watch JVM heap, GC, executor queue length and disk.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0225","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you integrate Jenkins metrics with Prometheus and Grafana?","a":"<p>Install the Prometheus metrics plugin, which exposes <code>/prometheus</code> for scraping. Grafana dashboards then show queue length, build durations, executor utilization, node health and JVM stats. Add alerts for queue backlog or controller memory.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0226","card":"q-jenkins-071-monitoring-and-troubleshooting","sec":"jenkins-s072","q":"How do you troubleshoot a hung or deadlocked Jenkins, or heavy GC pauses?","a":"<p>Capture thread dumps (<code>/threadDump</code>) to find blocked or deadlocked threads and heavy operations; capture a heap dump and enable GC logging to spot memory pressure. Common fixes: raise heap (<code>-Xmx</code>), tune GC (for example G1), reduce plugins and executors, move builds off the controller, add build discarders and split load across agents.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0227","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you manage disk space on the controller?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Build discarder</strong>: cap builds and artifacts kept per job (<code>buildDiscarder</code>).</li>\n  <li><strong>Artifact retention</strong>: keep artifacts for fewer builds than logs.</li>\n  <li>Clean workspaces (<code>cleanWs</code>), offload artifacts to external storage (Artifactory, Nexus, S3) and monitor <code>JENKINS_HOME</code> disk.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: &#x27;30&#x27;, artifactNumToKeepStr: &#x27;5&#x27;)) }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0228","card":"q-jenkins-072-disk-hygiene-and-workspace-cleanup","sec":"jenkins-s073","q":"How do you clean workspaces after a build?","a":"<p>Use the Workspace Cleanup plugin's <code>cleanWs</code> step, typically in <code>post { always { } }</code>, which frees disk and avoids stale files contaminating later builds.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post { always { cleanWs() } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0229","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate SonarQube and enforce quality gates?","a":"<p>Run the SonarScanner inside <code>withSonarQubeEnv</code>, then wait on the asynchronous quality gate with <code>waitForQualityGate</code> and fail the build if it is not passed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;SonarQube&#x27;) {\n  steps { withSonarQubeEnv(&#x27;sonar&#x27;) { sh &#x27;mvn sonar:sonar&#x27; } }\n}\nstage(&#x27;Quality Gate&#x27;) {\n  steps { timeout(time: 10, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n    waitForQualityGate abortPipeline: true    // fail if gate not met\n  } }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0230","card":"q-jenkins-073-quality-gates-and-vulnerability-scanning","sec":"jenkins-s074","q":"How do you integrate vulnerability scanners (Trivy, Snyk, Dependency-Check)?","a":"<p>Add a security stage that runs the scanner and fails on threshold breaches (for example high or critical CVEs). Publish reports for visibility.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Scan&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity HIGH,CRITICAL registry/app:$BUILD_NUMBER&#x27;\n    // sh &#x27;snyk test --severity-threshold=high&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0231","card":"q-jenkins-074-notifications","sec":"jenkins-s075","q":"How do you send Slack, Teams or email notifications by outcome?","a":"<p>Put them in the <code>post</code> block keyed to result conditions, using the Slack, Teams or Email Extension plugins.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>post {\n  success { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;good&#x27;,   message: &quot;✅ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot; }\n  failure { slackSend channel: &#x27;#ci&#x27;, color: &#x27;danger&#x27;, message: &quot;❌ ${env.JOB_NAME} #${env.BUILD_NUMBER} ${env.BUILD_URL}&quot; }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0232","card":"q-jenkins-075-scenario-controller-out-of-memory-from-p","sec":"jenkins-s076","q":"A commit triggers 10 parallel jobs and the controller crashes with OOM. How do you prevent and recover?","a":"<p>Recover by restarting the controller (temporarily with more <code>-Xmx</code>) and aborting the runaway builds. Prevent it by moving builds to agents (controller executors 0), capping concurrency with throttling, <code>disableConcurrentBuilds</code> and per-job or per-label limits, using a Quiet Period to coalesce rapid pushes, right-sizing executors, adding build discarders and alerting on heap and queue. Ephemeral Kubernetes agents keep load off the controller.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0233","card":"q-jenkins-076-scenario-a-pipeline-hangs-on-one-step","sec":"jenkins-s077","q":"A pipeline is stuck on a step indefinitely. How do you debug and safeguard?","a":"<p>Open the build's Console or Pipeline Steps to find the stuck step, take a controller thread dump (<code>/threadDump</code>) and check the agent (process, disk, network). Common culprits: a waiting <code>input</code>, a hung <code>sh</code> subprocess, network calls without timeouts, or an offline agent. Safeguard with <code>timeout</code> on risky steps, <code>retry</code> on flaky ones, an overall pipeline <code>timeout</code> option, and a timeout on <code>input</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>options { timeout(time: 60, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) }         // whole-pipeline guard\nsteps { timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) { sh &#x27;./maybe-hangs.sh&#x27; } }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0234","card":"q-jenkins-077-scenario-helm-deploy-without-embedding-a","sec":"jenkins-s078","q":"How do you deploy to Kubernetes with Helm without cluster-admin credentials in the codebase?","a":"<p>Store the kubeconfig or token as a Jenkins credential (better, use a cloud IAM/OIDC identity or a Vault-issued short-lived token), bind it only for the deploy block with <code>withCredentials</code>, and use a least-privilege service account scoped to the target namespace rather than cluster-admin. Run the deploy on an ephemeral agent and never commit secrets.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>withCredentials([file(credentialsId: &#x27;kubeconfig-prod&#x27;, variable: &#x27;KUBECONFIG&#x27;)]) {\n  sh &#x27;helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n app-prod --atomic --wait&#x27;\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0235","card":"q-jenkins-078-scenario-migrating-200-freestyle-jobs-to","sec":"jenkins-s079","q":"What is the step-by-step strategy?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Inventory and categorize jobs by pattern (build, deploy, cron).</li>\n  <li>Build templates and Shared Library steps for each common pattern.</li>\n  <li>Pilot-migrate a few representative jobs to Jenkinsfiles in their repos and run them in parallel with the old jobs to compare.</li>\n  <li>Roll out by category, converting configs to declarative plus Multibranch.</li>\n  <li>Standardize credentials through the credentials store and parameterize jobs.</li>\n  <li>Decommission Freestyle jobs once the pipelines are verified.</li>\n  <li>Add JCasC so the setup is reproducible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Migrate incrementally, never big-bang.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0236","card":"q-jenkins-079-scenario-blue-green-or-canary-deployment","sec":"jenkins-s080","q":"How do you design a zero-downtime blue/green or canary pipeline?","a":"<p><strong>Blue/green</strong>: deploy the new version to an idle green environment, run smoke tests, then switch the load balancer or service from blue to green (rollback is switching back). <strong>Canary</strong>: route a small percentage of traffic to the new version, watch metrics and error rates, then ramp up or roll back. In Jenkins use stages for deploy-green, test, <code>input</code> approval (or an automated metric gate), traffic shift and verify, with automatic rollback on failure.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Switch traffic&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    sh &#x27;kubectl apply -f green-deploy.yaml &amp;&amp; ./smoke-test.sh green&#x27;\n    input message: &#x27;Cut over to green?&#x27;, ok: &#x27;Switch&#x27;\n    sh &#x27;kubectl patch service app -p \\&#x27;{&quot;spec&quot;:{&quot;selector&quot;:{&quot;slot&quot;:&quot;green&quot;}}}\\&#x27;&#x27;\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"jenkins-f0237","card":"q-jenkins-080-scenario-flaky-scm-checkout","sec":"jenkins-s081","q":"How do you make checkout robust against intermittent network failures?","a":"<p>Wrap <code>checkout</code> in <code>retry</code> and <code>timeout</code>, log each attempt, and use <code>catchError</code> or <code>try</code> to surface diagnostics. Shallow clones and reasonable Git timeouts also reduce flakiness.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>stage(&#x27;Checkout&#x27;) {\n  steps {\n    retry(3) {\n      timeout(time: 5, unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27;) {\n        echo &quot;Checkout attempt at ${new Date()}&quot;\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":false}];
